;;! Structured tasks with typed results across the process boundary
;;! (R-C2, docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-13).
;
; WHAT IT IS. `taskMap f n width limit` runs `f i` for every `i` in
; `0 .. n`, each in a forked child, at most `width` at once, and answers
; one `(Result String Error)` per task in SUBMIT order. A task answers
; a `String`; what crosses back is its BYTES, copied out of the child
; into the parent's arena. That is the whole of the typed transfer: a
; program that wants a record, a `Vec`, a tree back encodes it into
; those bytes in the child and decodes them in the parent - JSON
; (`stdlib/Cereal.ax`) or a codec of its own. `tests/stdlib/542-task-codec.ax`
; carries a record with a `Vec` in it both ways and compares it with
; the sequential answer.
;
; WHY BYTES AND NOT A HEAP VALUE. A forked child's heap is a copy: a
; handle it answered would name the child's arena, which is gone once
; the child is (MM-PAR-3). No typed promotion of a heap value exists
; (MM-RGN-7 is planned), so serialization is not a workaround here, it
; is the transfer - explicit, bounded, and checked on arrival.
;
; A task is a caller's
; closure, and its captures are only isolated by the forked lowering
; (MM-PAR-3), which this primitive names whatever `--threads` says. So
; a `--threads` program's tasks are processes too, and a task's
; captured `Vec` is the child's own copy.
;
; EVERY FAILURE IS A VALUE, and each has its own code:
;
;   Err 22               the aligned slab size cannot fit in Int; no
;                        slab is mapped and no task is started
;   Ok s                 the task answered `s`, at most `limit` bytes
;   Err 1..255           the task trapped or died: its wait status, an
;                        exit code or 128 plus the signal (the word-pool
;                        convention); the runtime's message is on fd 2
;   Err taskTooLargeCode its answer was over `limit` bytes; nothing of
;                        it crossed, and the parent is unharmed
;   Err sysTimedOut      it ran past its deadline, and was SIGKILLed
;                        and reaped
;   Err taskCancelledCode the pool was cancelled: never started, or
;                        still running when the grace period ran out,
;                        and then SIGKILLed and reaped
;   Err 78 or 70         its spawn was refused - the kernel refused the
;                        fork or the handle table was full (78), or no
;                        page could be mapped for the handle (70) - and
;                        it never ran; the pool is then cancelled
;   Err 70               it ran out of memory: its `memory` budget, or
;                        the system's
;   Err 86               it ran out of stack: it touched the guard
;                        below the `stack` bytes it runs on
;   Err taskOverBudgetCode its answer would take the call past its
;                        `keep` budget; nothing of it is kept, and the
;                        pool is then cancelled
;
; The codes above 255 cannot be mistaken for a wait status.
;
; BOUNDED, ALL OF IT (MM-PAR-17). At most `width` children exist at
; once; the parent's per-slot state is a ring of `width` entries; each
; slot's answer is written into a MAP_SHARED slab of `width` slots of
; `limit` bytes, REUSED - O(width x limit), never O(n). Submission
; blocks while `width` tasks are outstanding: there is no queue. A
; task's slot is freed when its result is DELIVERED, in submit order,
; so a slow task holds back the tasks `width` places behind it (for the same reason: the answer order is the submit order, and
; a bounded buffer cannot hold results out of order for ever). The
; answer of `taskMap` is O(n) because it is n results; `keep` bounds
; the answer bytes it holds, and `taskFold` streams them instead, each
; one live only while the fold's step runs, so memory stays flat
; however many tasks go through. `memory` caps what one task's arena
; may map, so a task that runs away traps 70 in its own process.
; `stack` is the stack a task runs on, mapped in its own process with a
; guard below it (`__axiom_stack_run`), so it is the same size whichever
; thread started the pool. Native entry and worker stacks follow
; RLIMIT_STACK; a task that exceeds its own budget traps 86.
;
; DEADLINES, CANCELLATION, FAILURE.
;   * `deadline` (nanoseconds, 0 for none) runs from a task's spawn. A
;     task past it is sent SIGKILL and reaped, and answers
;     `sysTimedOut`; if it had already finished or died of its own
;     accord, that answer wins.
;   * A TOKEN is a `CancelToken` (`taskTokenNew`): a sealed owner
;     (docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-16) of one shared page, unmapped
;     when its last owner in this address space lets go.
;     `taskCancel` sets it from anywhere - another binding, a task, the
;     pool itself - and wakes every pool sleeping on it;
;     `taskCancelled` is the poll a cooperative task makes. It is
;     declared `shared`, so a `parallel` form lends it to its bindings.
;     The pool checks it between every spawn attempt. Once it sees the
;     flag set, it starts nothing more (every unstarted task answers `taskCancelledCode`),
;     gives running tasks `grace` nanoseconds to finish, then SIGKILLs
;     and reaps what is left. A token the caller passes is shared state:
;     cancelling it cancels every pool using it.
;   * `failFast` sets the pool's token at the first task that answers
;     an error (not at a cancellation), which cancels the rest as above.
;   * A SPAWN THAT IS REFUSED cancels the pool too, without touching the
;     token: the refused task answers the refusal's status (78, or 70),
;     nothing more starts, running tasks get `grace` and are then
;     SIGKILLed and reaped, and the pool returns through its normal
;     path, so the slab is unmapped and a private token let go. The spawn
;     runs inside a recovery point of its own (`taskSpawn`), which is
;     how the runtime's refusal - a trap, MM-PAR-7 - comes back here as
;     a status instead of unwinding past the pool's cleanup.
;
; NO CHILD OUTLIVES THE CALL on any path the program has. A normal
; return joins every child it spawned. A trap in the parent during the
; pool - `taskFold`'s step, say - is MM-PAR-7's: every child is on the
; spawning thread's registry, and the unrecovered trap, or the recovery
; point armed outside the pool, SIGKILLs and reaps them
; (`scripts/check-task.sh` records the pids and requires them gone).
; What this cannot cover, stated: a parent killed by a signal it does
; not handle runs no sweep, so its tasks are reparented; a pool
; running inside a `parallel` binding that MM-PAR-7's sweep SIGKILLs
; cannot sweep its own children (MM-PAR-7's grandchildren limit); and
; under `--threads` the registry is per thread, so a pool's tasks
; outlive a trap in a SIBLING thread, which ends the process with only
; its own registry swept (and a raw `sysExitWith` sweeps nothing).
;
; HOW THE PARENT WAITS. It sleeps on the token's event counter
; (`sysWaitWordTimeout`), which a task bumps when its answer is in the
; slab and `taskCancel` bumps when it cancels - so a normal completion
; or a cancellation wakes it at once. A task that dies WITHOUT
; answering (a trap, a signal) bumps nothing, so the parent also looks
; at each running child every `taskPollNanos` (10 ms) with
; `sysChildExited` - `waitid` with WNOWAIT, which does not reap, so the
; join that follows still owns its child. Where no such look exists
; (`sysChildExited` answers `Err`), a death without an answer is found
; at the task's deadline, or - with no deadline - by blocking on the
; oldest running task's join when nothing else can move, and a
; cancellation then waits for that join.
;
; A TASK IS KILLED BY ITS PID, which `__spawn_pid` reads from the page
; its `Spawn` handle names, through the runtime's handle table
; (MM-PAR-8): the page layout stays the runtime's, and a handle already
; joined traps with status 85 instead of reading an unmapped page.
; `scripts/check-task.sh` checks the pid through the deadline's kill:
; with any other number, the task outlives its deadline and the check
; goes red (the `layout` ablation).
;
; DETERMINISM. The RESULTS are in submit order and depend only on what
; each task answered - never on scheduling. SIDE EFFECTS are not: tasks
; run concurrently, so their writes to fd 1, to files or to a shared
; mapping interleave however the scheduler ran them, and a deadline or
; cancellation decides by the clock. That clock is the monotonic one
; (`sysTimeoutMicros`) everywhere but darwin-x86_64, where it is the
; realtime one and a step of it moves every running deadline by the
; step.
(import Internal.Raw)
(import Internal.RawVec)
(import Internal.TaskWords)
(import Internal.Command)
(import Internal.CommandPool)
(import Str)
(import Vec)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; ---- the options ---------------------------------------------------------
;; `width` children at most (clamped to 1..n); `limit` bytes per answer
;; (0 or more); `deadline` nanoseconds per task from its spawn, 0 for
;; none; `grace` nanoseconds a running task gets after a cancellation;
;; `failFast` cancels the pool at the first error; `token` is `Some` a
;; token from `taskTokenNew`, or `None` for one private to the call;
;; `keep` bytes of answers one call may hold, and `memory` bytes one
;; task's arena may map, each 0 for no bound; `stack` bytes of stack
;; each task runs on, 0 for `taskStackDefault`.
(pub struct TaskOpts
  (width : Int)
  (limit : Int)
  (deadline : Int)
  (grace : Int)
  (failFast : Bool)
  (token : (Option CancelToken))
  (keep : Int)
  (memory : Int)
  (stack : Int))

;; No deadline, a 100 ms grace, no fail-fast, a private token, no `keep`
;; or `memory` bound, and the default stack.
(pub :: taskOpts (-> Int Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskOpts width limit)
  (TaskOpts
    :width width
    :limit limit
    :deadline 0
    :grace 100000000
    :failFast false
    :token None
    :keep 0
    :memory 0
    :stack 0))

;; The options with a deadline of `nanos` nanoseconds for each task,
;; measured from that task's spawn.
(pub :: taskWithDeadline (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithDeadline o nanos)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline nanos
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token o.token
    :keep o.keep
    :memory o.memory
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with `nanos` nanoseconds of grace after cancellation
;; before a task is abandoned.
(pub :: taskWithGrace (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithGrace o nanos)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace nanos
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token o.token
    :keep o.keep
    :memory o.memory
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with fail-fast `on` or off: when on, the first failure
;; cancels the rest.
(pub :: taskWithFailFast (-> TaskOpts Bool TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithFailFast o on)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast on
    :token o.token
    :keep o.keep
    :memory o.memory
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with `tok` as the cancel token: cancelling it cancels
;; the run.
(pub :: taskWithToken (-> TaskOpts CancelToken TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithToken o tok)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token (Some tok)
    :keep o.keep
    :memory o.memory
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with a `keep` budget of `bytes`: the answers one call
;; holds may total at most that many bytes. The answer that would pass
;; it answers `Err taskOverBudgetCode` instead, and the pool is then
;; cancelled as a refused spawn cancels it. `taskFold` holds no answer,
;; so the budget doesn't bound it. 0 is no bound.
(pub :: taskWithKeep (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithKeep o bytes)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token o.token
    :keep bytes
    :memory o.memory
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with a `memory` budget of `bytes`: each task's arena may
;; map at most that many bytes beyond what its process began with. A
;; task that asks for more traps 70 in its own process and answers
;; `Err 70`; its siblings and the caller are untouched. 0 is no bound.
(pub :: taskWithMemory (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithMemory o bytes)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token o.token
    :keep o.keep
    :memory bytes
    :stack o.stack))

;; The options with a `stack` budget of `bytes`: each task runs on a
;; stack of that many bytes, rounded up to 64 KiB, mapped in its own
;; process with a guard below it, whichever thread started the pool. A
;; task that recurses past it traps 86 in its own process and answers
;; `Err 86`; its siblings and the caller are untouched. 0 or less is
;; `taskStackDefault`.
(pub :: taskWithStack (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithStack o bytes)
  (TaskOpts
    :width o.width
    :limit o.limit
    :deadline o.deadline
    :grace o.grace
    :failFast o.failFast
    :token o.token
    :keep o.keep
    :memory o.memory
    :stack bytes))

;; The stack a task runs on when its options name none: 8 MiB, what
;; Linux and Darwin give a main thread by default.
(pub :: taskStackDefault Int)
(pub fn (taskStackDefault)
  8388608)

; ---- the codes -------------------------------------------------------------
;; The error code a cancelled task answers, 1002.
(pub :: taskCancelledCode Int)
(pub fn (taskCancelledCode)
  1002)

;; The error code for a task whose input is larger than the budget
;; allows, 1003.
(pub :: taskTooLargeCode Int)
(pub fn (taskTooLargeCode)
  1003)

;; The error code a task answers when its answer would take the call
;; past its `keep` budget, 1012.
(pub :: taskOverBudgetCode Int)
(pub fn (taskOverBudgetCode)
  1012)

;; How often a sleeping pool looks at its running children: 10 ms.
(pub :: taskPollNanos Int)
(pub fn (taskPollNanos)
  10000000)

; The first look after a task has answered and not yet exited: 20 us.
; Its process is normally microseconds from its `exit`, so a fixed
; millisecond here held every single-task pool for one (a p50 round
; trip of 1.4 ms against 0.24 ms before, `scripts/bench-concurrency.sh`).
; Each look that finds nothing doubles it, up to `taskPollNanos`, so a
; task that answered and cannot exit costs no busy loop while its
; deadline runs.
(:: taskExitNapFirst Int)
(fn (taskExitNapFirst)
  20000)

(:: taskNapNext (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskNapNext nap)
  (if (> (* nap 2) taskPollNanos)
    taskPollNanos
    (* nap 2)))

; The poll period: `nap` while some task has answered and not yet exited
; (`answered` 1), else the usual one.
(:: taskPollPeriod (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskPollPeriod answered nap)
  (if (== answered 1)
    nap
    taskPollNanos))

; ---- the raw words ---------------------------------------------------------
; The module's only raw accesses, each `effect(unsafe)`; every public
; function goes through them and claims no more than `effect(io)`.
(:: taskLoadAt (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskLoadAt a)
  (__atomic_load a))

(:: taskStoreAt (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskStoreAt a v)
  {
    (__atomic_store a v)
    0
  })

(:: taskAddAt (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskAddAt a v)
  (__atomic_add a v))

; The pid a task runs as, read through its live `Spawn` handle.
(:: taskHandlePid (-> Spawn Int))
(fn (taskHandlePid h)
  (__spawn_pid h))

; A block with a share taken, and the share given back.
(:: taskScratch (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskScratch n)
  {
    (let b (rawAlloc n))
    {
      (__retain b)
      b
    }
  })

(:: taskScratchDone (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskScratchDone b)
  {
    (__release b)
    0
  })

; ---- the token -------------------------------------------------------------
;; A cancellation token: one owner whose value is a shared page - word 0
;; the cancelled flag, word 1 the event counter a pool sleeps on
;; (MM-PAR-16).
(pub struct CancelToken sealed shared
  (owner : Handle))

; The page a token names. Its owner is live while any caller holds the
; token, so the page is mapped.
(:: taskTokenAt (-> CancelToken Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenAt t)
  (__resource_get t.owner))

;; A fresh token, not set, or `Err` the mapping's error.
(pub :: taskTokenNew (Result CancelToken Error))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (taskTokenNew)
  (match (sysMapShared 4096)
    ((Ok page) (Ok (taskTokenOwned page)))
    ((Err e) (Err e))))

; The owner of a fresh page, whose cleanup unmaps it.
(:: taskTokenOwned (-> Int CancelToken))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenOwned page)
  (CancelToken :owner (__resource_new taskTokenDrop page)))

; The cleanup of a token's last owner in this address space: unmap this
; address space's view of the page. Answers 0, or the negated errno of
; an unmap that failed.
(:: taskTokenDrop (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskTokenDrop page)
  (match (sysUnmapShared page 4096)
    ((Ok z) 0)
    ((Err e) (- 0 (errCode e)))))

;; Set the token and wake every pool sleeping on it. Idempotent.
(pub :: taskCancel (-> CancelToken Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (taskCancel t)
  (taskCancelAt (taskTokenAt t)))

;; Whether the token is set: the poll a cooperative task makes.
(pub :: taskCancelled (-> CancelToken Bool))
(pub fn (taskCancelled t)
  (taskCancelledAt (taskTokenAt t)))

; The two halves above, on the page itself: what the pool and its tasks
; use once the token's page is in hand.
(:: taskCancelAt (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskCancelAt tok)
  {
    (taskStoreAt tok 1)
    (taskBump tok)
  })

(:: taskCancelledAt (-> Int Bool))
(fn (taskCancelledAt tok)
  (== (taskLoadAt tok) 1))

(:: taskBump (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskBump tok)
  {
    (taskAddAt (+ tok 8) 1)
    (sysWakeWord (+ tok 8))
  })

(:: taskEvent (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskEvent tok)
  (taskLoadAt (+ tok 8)))

; ---- the child's half ----------------------------------------------------
; Cap this process's arena at `bytes` beyond what it has mapped now, so
; a task that asks for more traps 70 (MM-PAR-17). 0 is no cap, and a
; cap past the largest `Int` saturates.
(:: taskCapArena (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskCapArena bytes)
  (if (> bytes 0)
    {
      (let now (__axiom_mem_stat 2))
      (__axiom_arena_cap
        (if (> bytes (- 9223372036854775807 now))
          9223372036854775807
          (+ now bytes)))
    }
    0))

; A slot of the slab: word 0 the state (1 running, 2 answered, 3 over
; the limit), word 1 the answer's length, word 2 the child's pid as the
; child saw it, then the answer's bytes from byte 32.
;
; `arg` is `t * w + s`: task `t` in slot `s`, one word because a spawn
; hands its thunk one. Runs in the CHILD; the state is stored after the
; bytes, and the parent reads the bytes only after the join, which
; orders them (MM-PAR-9, edge 3) - the state is the wake-up, not the
; publication.
;
; The task runs on a stack of `stack` bytes this process maps, with a
; guard below it (`__axiom_stack_run`, MM-PAR-17), so its stack is its
; budget and not the stack of whichever thread forked it, and a task
; that recurses past it traps 86 in this process and nowhere else.
(:: taskChild (-> (-> Int String) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskChild f slab slotBytes limit tok w memory stack arg)
  {
    (let slot (+ slab (* (% arg w) slotBytes)))
    {
      (rawSetWord slot 2 sysPid)
      (taskCapArena memory)
      (__axiom_stack_run stack (lambda (i) (taskAnswer f slot limit tok i)) arg)
    }
  })

; Run task `i` and write its answer into `slot`: the length, then the
; bytes and the answered state, or the over-the-limit state alone.
(:: taskAnswer (-> (-> Int String) Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskAnswer f slot limit tok i)
  {
    (let r (f i))
    {
      (let len (strLen r))
      {
        (rawSetWord slot 1 len)
        (if (> len limit)
          (taskStoreAt slot 3)
          {
            (rawCopy (+ slot 32) (strData r) len)
            (taskStoreAt slot 2)
          })
        (taskBump tok)
        0
      }
    }
  })

; ---- the parent's half ---------------------------------------------------
; Per slot, three words of `st`: the deadline (microseconds on
; `sysTimeoutMicros`'s clock, 0 for none), the wait status, and why it
; was joined - 0 not yet, 1 it ended, 2 killed at its deadline, 3 killed
; at the end of a cancellation's grace. The slot's `Spawn` handle is
; word `s` of `hs`.
(:: taskSt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSt st s k)
  (vecGet st (+ (* 3 s) k)))

(:: taskSetSt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSetSt st s k v)
  {
    (vecSet st (+ (* 3 s) k) v)
    0
  })

; Join slot `s`'s child - it has ended, or was just killed - and record
; why. Answers 1 when the outcome is an error `failFast` counts: not a
; cancellation's kill.
(:: taskJoin (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes why)
  {
    (let w (__proc_join_nr (vecGet hs s) cell))
    {
      (let status (rawGetWord cell 0))
      (let state (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))))
      {
        (taskSetSt st s 1 status)
        (taskSetSt st s 2 why)
        ; The same `finished` test `taskResultAt` makes: a task that
        ; answered and was then SIGKILLed from outside (137, joined as
        ; ended) is delivered as `Err 137`, so it counts for `failFast`.
        (if (== why 3)
          0
          (if (&& (== state 2) (|| (== status 0) (&& (== status 137) (>= why 2))))
            0
            1))
      }
    }
  })

; Kill slot `s`'s child and join it.
(:: taskKillJoin (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskKillJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes why)
  {
    (match (sysKill (taskHandlePid (vecGet hs s)) 9)
      ((Ok z) ())
      ((Err e) ()))
    (taskJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes why)
  })

; The answer of a joined slot, built in the caller's current region.
; `slot` is the slab slot, `status` the joined child's wait status and
; `why` why it was joined (`taskJoin`), or 4 when its spawn was refused
; and `status` is the refusal's.
(:: taskResultAt (-> Int Int Int (Result String Error)))
(fn (taskResultAt slot status why)
  {
    (let state (taskLoadAt slot))
    (let finished (|| (== status 0) (&& (== status 137) (>= why 2))))
    (if (== why 4)
      (Err (mkError status "task not started: its spawn was refused"))
      (if (&& finished (== state 2))
        (Ok (taskCopyOut slot))
        (if (&& finished (== state 3))
          (Err (mkError taskTooLargeCode "task answer over the byte limit"))
          (if (== status 0)
            (Err (mkError 78 "task ended without an answer"))
            (if (&& (== status 137) (== why 2))
              (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "task past its deadline"))
              (if (&& (== status 137) (== why 3))
                (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task cancelled"))
                (if (== status 70)
                  (Err (mkError 70 "task out of memory"))
                  (if (== status 86)
                    (Err (mkError 86 "task out of stack"))
                    (Err (mkError status "task trapped"))))))))))
  })

; The bytes slot `s`'s answer keeps once delivered: its length when it
; is an `Ok` (`taskResultAt`'s test), else 0.
(:: taskOkBytes (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int))
(fn (taskOkBytes st s slab slotBytes)
  {
    (let slot (+ slab (* s slotBytes)))
    (let status (taskSt st s 1))
    (let why (taskSt st s 2))
    (if (&& (!= why 4)
          (&& (|| (== status 0) (&& (== status 137) (>= why 2))) (== (taskLoadAt slot) 2)))
      (rawGetWord slot 1)
      0)
  })

(:: taskCopyOut (-> Int String))
(fn (taskCopyOut slot)
  {
    (let len (rawGetWord slot 1))
    {
      (let s (strAlloc len))
      {
        (rawCopy (strData s) (+ slot 32) len)
        s
      }
    }
  })

; `scoped` is zero for retained answers, or a retained four-word
; mark cell for taskFold. The pool and its captures predate each mark.
; The fold's caller promises that the sink retains no scoped allocation.
; Refreshing the cell allocates nothing, so deliveries leave no marks.
(:: taskDeliver (-> (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskDeliver sink scoped st s slab slotBytes)
  {
    (let slot (+ slab (* s slotBytes)))
    (let status (taskSt st s 1))
    (let why (taskSt st s 2))
    (if (!= scoped 0)
      {
        (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
        (sink (taskResultAt slot status why))
        (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
      }
      (sink (taskResultAt slot status why)))
  })

; Deliver slot `s` against the `keep` budget: `kept` is what the call
; holds so far, or -1 once an answer passed the budget. An answer that
; would pass it is delivered as `Err taskOverBudgetCode`, and so is
; every non-empty `Ok` after it. Answers the new `kept`. `keep` 0 is no budget,
; and the fold (`scoped` non-zero) keeps nothing, so neither counts.
(:: taskDeliverKept (-> (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskDeliverKept sink scoped st s slab slotBytes keep kept)
  (if (|| (== keep 0) (!= scoped 0))
    {
      (taskDeliver sink scoped st s slab slotBytes)
      kept
    }
    {
      (let n (taskOkBytes st s slab slotBytes))
      (if (== n 0)
        {
          (taskDeliver sink scoped st s slab slotBytes)
          kept
        }
        (if (|| (< kept 0) (> n (- keep kept)))
          {
            (sink (Err (mkError taskOverBudgetCode "task answer past the call's keep budget")))
            (- 0 1)
          }
          {
            (taskDeliver sink scoped st s slab slotBytes)
            (+ kept n)
          }))
    }))

(:: taskDeliverCancelled (-> (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskDeliverCancelled sink scoped)
  (if (!= scoped 0)
    {
      (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
      (sink (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task not started: the pool was cancelled")))
      (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
    }
    (sink (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task not started: the pool was cancelled")))))

; Earliest instant, in microseconds, the parent must wake by: the
; nearest deadline of a running task, the end of a cancellation's
; grace, or `now` plus the poll period - or -1 when none applies. A task
; that has answered and not yet exited is normally a few microseconds
; from its exit, so while one exists the period is `nap`, the backoff
; `taskLoop` keeps.
(:: taskWakeBy (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
(fn (taskWakeBy st head next w now canPoll cancelling graceEnd slab slotBytes nap)
  {
    (let mut wake
      (if (== cancelling 1)
        graceEnd
        (- 0 1)))
    (let mut running 0)
    (let mut answered 0)
    {
      (for t in head..next
        {
          (let s (% t w))
          (if (== (taskSt st s 2) 0)
            {
              (set running 1)
              (if (>= (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))) 2)
                (set answered 1))
              {
                (let d (taskSt st s 0))
                (if (&& (> d 0) (|| (< wake 0) (< d wake)))
                  (set wake d))
              }
            })
        })
      (if (== running 0)
        (- 0 1)
        (if (== canPoll 1)
          {
            (let p (+ now (/ (taskPollPeriod answered nap) 1000)))
            (if (|| (< wake 0) (< p wake))
              p
              wake)
          }
          wake))
    }
  })

; Has slot `s`'s child ended? 1 yes - the kernel says it has exited, or
; it answered and there is no look to ask - 2 it answered but has not
; exited yet, 0 not seen, -1 the look itself failed (`sysChildExited`
; answered `Err`; the caller stops asking). An answer alone is not an
; end: a task whose `f` returned can still be held up - by a thread its
; own `parallel` never joins, say - and a join then would block the
; whole pool in the kernel with no deadline or grace enforced. So an
; answered task is joined when the kernel reports its exit, and until
; then stays under its deadline and a cancellation's grace like any
; other running task.
(:: taskEnded (-> (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskEnded hs s slab slotBytes pbuf canPoll)
  {
    (let answered (>= (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))) 2))
    (if (== canPoll 1)
      (match (sysChildExited (taskHandlePid (vecGet hs s)) pbuf)
        ((Ok b)
          (if b
            1
            (if answered
              2
              0))
        )
        ((Err e)
          (if answered
            1
            (- 0 1))
        ))
      (if answered
        1
        0))
  })

; Look at running slot `s` once, at `now`: join it if it ended, kill
; and join it if its deadline or a cancellation's grace has run out.
; Answers -1 nothing happened, -2 the look at the child failed, else
; `taskJoin`'s answer (1 an error `failFast` counts, 0 not).
(:: taskCheck (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskCheck st hs s now cancelling graceEnd canPoll cell slab slotBytes pbuf)
  {
    (let ended (taskEnded hs s slab slotBytes pbuf canPoll))
    (if (== ended 1)
      (taskJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes 1)
      (if (&& (> (taskSt st s 0) 0) (>= now (taskSt st s 0)))
        (taskKillJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes 2)
        (if (&& (== cancelling 1) (>= now graceEnd))
          (taskKillJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes 3)
          (if (== ended (- 0 1))
            (- 0 2)
            (- 0 1)))))
  })

; Spawn `wrapper` on `arg` inside a recovery point of its own, so a
; refused spawn comes back here as a status rather than trapping past
; the pool (MM-PAR-7's 78). Answers the handle's word, or the status:
; 78 when the kernel refused the fork or the handle table was full, 70
; when the handle's page could not be mapped. The runtime has already
; given back the page and the thunk's share by then, and a child
; spawned in the extent is swept by the abort, but none can be: the
; spawn is all it does.
;
; WHERE THE MARK COMES FROM depends on the pool. An arena reset empties
; the allocator's free lists, so in a pool that keeps its answers
; (`mark` non-zero) a `region` around each spawn lost to the arena
; whatever the pool had given back since the last one - an answer's
; copy, a scratch cell, the spawn's own closure: about 110 bytes a task,
; measured, for the life of the process. There the recovery point's
; mark is `mark`, a cell the pool keeps and this call refreshes, which
; allocates nothing, and the closure is given back to be reused. A fold
; (`mark` 0) resets the arena at every delivery anyway, so a `region`
; reclaims the closure outright rather than leaving it to that reset.
(:: taskSpawn (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskSpawn wrapper arg mark)
  (if (== mark 0)
    (region r
      (__axiom_recover __axiom_arena_mark (lambda (x) (taskWordOf (__proc_spawn wrapper arg)))))
    {
      (__axiom_arena_mark_into mark)
      (__axiom_recover mark (lambda (x) (taskWordOf (__proc_spawn wrapper arg))))
    }))

; Whether `taskSpawn` answered a handle: a handle's word is its slot's
; generation, at least 1, times 2^16 plus the slot, and a status is
; below 256.
(:: taskSpawned (-> Int Bool))
(fn (taskSpawned hw)
  (>= hw 65536))

; A spawn handle as the word the recovery point can answer, and back.
; The way back makes a `Spawn` out of a word, which is a forging cast
; (docs/memory-model.md MM-EXEC-9d), so it says `effect(unsafe)`: the
; word must be one `taskSpawn` answered and `taskSpawned` accepted. The
; precondition checks the half a word can show, that it is no status;
; the handle table checks the rest at the join, which traps 85 on a
; word no spawn answered (MM-PAR-8). This is the module's one such
; cast.
(:: taskWordOf (-> Spawn Int))
(fn (taskWordOf h)
  (as h Int))

(:: taskHandleOf (-> Int Spawn))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:pre((>= hw 65536))
(fn (taskHandleOf hw)
  (as hw Spawn))

; Start task `t` in its slot `t % w`. Its deadline runs from the clock
; read AFTER the spawn answered, so a task started late in a batch -
; after other forks - keeps the whole of its deadline. Slot `s`'s first
; task pushes its handle onto `hs`, which grows to `w` in slot order;
; every later one replaces the handle joined before it. Answers 0, or
; the status of a refused spawn, which the slot then holds as joined
; with `why` 4: nothing ran, and `hs` is not touched.
(:: taskStart (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) (-> Int Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
(fn (taskStart st hs wrapper mark t w slab slotBytes deadlineUs)
  {
    (let s (% t w))
    (let slot (+ slab (* (% t w) slotBytes)))
    {
      (taskStoreAt slot 1)
      (rawSetWord slot 1 0)
      (rawSetWord slot 2 0)
      ; The child derives its ring slot from the index. Packing both
      ; into one word would overflow for large indices or widths.
      {
        (let hw (taskSpawn wrapper t mark))
        (if (taskSpawned hw)
          {
            {
              (let h (taskHandleOf hw))
              (if (< t w)
                (vecPush hs h)
                (vecSet hs s h))
            }
            (taskSetSt st s 0
              (if (> deadlineUs 0)
                (+ sysTimeoutMicros deadlineUs)
                0))
            (taskSetSt st s 1 0)
            (taskSetSt st s 2 0)
            0
          }
          {
            (taskSetSt st s 0 0)
            (taskSetSt st s 1 hw)
            (taskSetSt st s 2 4)
            hw
          })
      }
    }
  })

; Nanoseconds from `now` to `wake` (microseconds), at least one, and at
; most 2^62: the product must not wrap into a negative sleep.
(:: taskSleepFor (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSleepFor now wake)
  (if (> wake now)
    (if (> (- wake now) 4611686018427387)
      4611686018427387000
      (* (- wake now) 1000))
    1000))

; Microseconds for a nanosecond option, rounded up; 0 stays 0. A value
; within 999 of the largest `Int` must not wrap as it rounds - a grace
; of "for ever" would read as a negative one and kill at once.
(:: taskMicros (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskMicros nanos)
  (if (> nanos 0)
    (if (> nanos 9223372036854774808)
      9223372036854776
      (/ (+ nanos 999) 1000))
    0))

; The pool itself. Answers how many results it delivered (n).
(:: taskLoop
  (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskLoop f n opts sink scoped w limit slotBytes tok slab)
  {
    (let st (vecWithCapacity (* 3 w)))
    (let hs (vecWithCapacity w))
    (let cell (taskScratch 8))
    (let pbuf (taskScratch sysChildPollBytes))
    (let memory opts.memory)
    (let stack
      (if (> opts.stack 0)
        opts.stack
        taskStackDefault))
    (let keep
      (if (< opts.keep 0)
        0
        opts.keep))
    (let wrapper (lambda (arg) (taskChild f slab slotBytes limit tok w memory stack arg)))
    (let spawnMark
      (if (== scoped 0)
        (taskScratch 32)
        0))
    (let deadlineUs (taskMicros opts.deadline))
    (let graceUs (taskMicros opts.grace))
    (let mut kept 0)
    (let mut next 0)
    (let mut head 0)
    (let mut cancelling 0)
    (let mut graceEnd 0)
    (let mut canPoll 1)
    (let mut progress 0)
    (let mut nap taskExitNapFirst)
    {
      (for i in 0..(* 3 w)
        (vecPush st 0))
      (while (< head n)
        {
          (let seen (taskEvent tok))
          (let now sysTimeoutMicros)
          {
            (set progress 0)
            ; A cancellation seen for the first time.
            (if (&& (== cancelling 0) (taskCancelledAt tok))
              {
                (set cancelling 1)
                (set graceEnd (+ now graceUs))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Start what the window allows. A refused spawn cancels the
            ; pool as a cancellation does, and leaves the token alone.
            (while (&& (== cancelling 0)
                     (&& (! (taskCancelledAt tok)) (&& (< next n) (< (- next head) w))))
              {
                (if (!= (taskStart st hs wrapper spawnMark next w slab slotBytes deadlineUs) 0)
                  {
                    (set cancelling 1)
                    (set graceEnd (+ sysTimeoutMicros graceUs))
                  })
                (set next (+ next 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Cancellation may arrive during the launch window. Observe it
            ; before joining or delivering, and grant grace from this point.
            (if (&& (== cancelling 0) (taskCancelledAt tok))
              {
                (set cancelling 1)
                (set graceEnd (+ sysTimeoutMicros graceUs))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Join what has ended; kill and join what ran out of time.
            (for t in head..next
              {
                (let s (% t w))
                (if (== (taskSt st s 2) 0)
                  {
                    (let r
                      (taskCheck st hs s now cancelling graceEnd canPoll cell slab slotBytes pbuf))
                    (if (== r (- 0 2))
                      (set canPoll 0)
                      (if (>= r 0)
                        {
                          (set progress 1)
                          (if (&& (== r 1) opts.failFast)
                            (taskCancelAt tok))
                        }))
                  })
              })
            ; Deliver, in submit order, what has been joined. An answer
            ; past the `keep` budget cancels the pool as a refused spawn
            ; does, and leaves the token alone.
            (while (&& (< head next) (!= (taskSt st (% head w) 2) 0))
              {
                {
                  (let k (taskDeliverKept sink scoped st (% head w) slab slotBytes keep kept))
                  {
                    (if (&& (< k 0) (&& (>= kept 0) (== cancelling 0)))
                      {
                        (set cancelling 1)
                        (set graceEnd (+ sysTimeoutMicros graceUs))
                      })
                    (set kept k)
                  }
                }
                (set head (+ head 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Once cancelled, what was never started answers so.
            (while (&& (== cancelling 1) (&& (== head next) (< next n)))
              {
                (taskDeliverCancelled sink scoped)
                (set next (+ next 1))
                (set head (+ head 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Nothing moved: sleep until something can. The exit backoff
            ; grows with each empty look and starts again on progress.
            (if (== progress 0)
              {
                (let wake
                  (taskWakeBy st head next w now canPoll cancelling graceEnd slab slotBytes nap))
                {
                  (set nap (taskNapNext nap))
                  (if (>= wake 0)
                    (sysWaitWordTimeout (+ tok 8) seen (taskSleepFor now wake))
                    ; No clock can end the wait and no look at a child
                    ; exists: block on the oldest running task's join.
                    (if (&& (< head next) (== (taskSt st (% head w) 2) 0))
                      {
                        (let r (taskJoin st hs (% head w) cell slab slotBytes 1))
                        (if (&& (== r 1) opts.failFast)
                          (taskCancelAt tok))
                      }))
                }
              }
              (set nap taskExitNapFirst))
          }
        })
      (taskScratchDone cell)
      (taskScratchDone pbuf)
      (if (!= spawnMark 0)
        (taskScratchDone spawnMark))
      head
    }
  })

; Every task answers `e`: the pool could not be set up.
(:: taskAllFailed (-> Int Error (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped)
  {
    (for i in 0..n
      (if (!= scoped 0)
        {
          (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
          (sink (Err e))
          (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
        }
        (sink (Err e))))
    n
  })

; The pool with its slab mapped around it.
(:: taskWithSlab
  (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskWithSlab f n opts sink scoped w limit slotBytes tok)
  (if (> w (/ 9223372036854775807 slotBytes))
    (taskAllFailed n (mkError 22 "task slab size exceeds Int range") sink scoped)
    {
      (let bytes (* w slotBytes))
      (match (sysMapShared bytes)
        ((Err e) (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped))
        ((Ok slab)
          {
            (let done (taskLoop f n opts sink scoped w limit slotBytes tok slab))
            (match (sysUnmapShared slab bytes)
              ((Ok z) done)
              ((Err e) done))
          }
        ))
    }))

; The token the pool sleeps on: the caller's, or a fresh one.
(:: taskTokenFor (-> (Option CancelToken) (Result CancelToken Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskTokenFor tok)
  (match tok
    ((Some t) (Ok t))
    ((None) taskTokenNew)))

; The width actually used: at least 1, at most n.
(:: taskWidth (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskWidth width n)
  (if (< width 1)
    1
    (if (> width n)
      n
      width)))

; The pool with its mappings made and unmade around it.
(:: taskRun (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskRun f n opts sink scoped)
  (if (<= n 0)
    0
    {
      (let w (taskWidth opts.width n))
      (let limit
        (if (< opts.limit 0)
          0
          opts.limit))
      (if (> limit 1099511627776)
        (taskAllFailed n (mkError 22 "task limit over 2^40 bytes") sink scoped)
        (match (taskTokenFor opts.token)
          ((Err e) (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped))
          ; `tok` is held until the pool returns, so the page its tasks
          ; and its waits use stays mapped, and a private token is let go
          ; with this arm. The call is not in tail position, so no jump
          ; ends the arm's scope before the pool does.
          ((Ok tok)
            {
              (let done
                (taskWithSlab f n opts sink scoped w limit (+ 32 (* 8 (/ (+ limit 7) 8)))
                  (taskTokenAt tok)))
              done
            }
          )))
    }))

; ---- the entries ---------------------------------------------------------
;; `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once, each answer
;; at most `limit` bytes: one `Result` per task in submit order. No
;; deadline, no fail-fast, a private token.
(pub :: taskMap (-> (-> Int String) Int Int Int (Vec (Result String Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMap f n width limit)
  (taskMapWith f n (taskOpts width limit)))

;; `taskMap` with every option (`TaskOpts`).
(pub :: taskMapWith (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result String Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapWith f n opts)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (taskRun f n opts
        (lambda (r)
          {
            (vecPush out r)
            0
          }
        )
        0)
      out
    }
  })

;; Run `worker start end` on contiguous half-open ranges covering
;; `0 .. count`. `grain` is the maximum items per range, clamped to one.
;; Each range runs in a fresh child and answers one `Result` in range
;; order. Captures are isolated between ranges; work inside one range
;; shares that child's state. All options and budgets apply per range.
(pub :: taskMapRanges (-> (-> Int Int String) Int Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result String Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapRanges worker count grain opts)
  (if (<= count 0)
    vecNew
    {
      (let size
        (if (< grain 1)
          1
          grain))
      (taskMapWith
        (lambda (i)
          {
            (let start (* i size))
            {
              (let remaining (- count start))
              (worker start
                (+ start
                  (if (< remaining size)
                    remaining
                    size)))
            }
          }
        )
        (+ 1 (/ (- count 1) size)) opts)
    }))

;; Decode each successful answer in the parent, in submit order. Task
;; failures pass through unchanged and never call `decode`. The encoded
;; answers keep `taskMap`'s byte limit; decoded storage is the decoder's
;; responsibility and the result vector holds n values.
(pub :: taskMapDecoded
  (-> (-> Int String) (-> String (Result a Error)) Int Int Int (Vec (Result a Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapDecoded f decode n width limit)
  (taskMapDecodedWith f decode n (taskOpts width limit)))

;; All task options, with parent-side decoding. Decoder errors belong to
;; their result slots; `failFast` concerns worker failures, not decoding.
;; Delivery calls `decode` before starting more work, so a slow decoder
;; can delay submission and deadline/cancellation handling.
(pub :: taskMapDecodedWith
  (-> (-> Int String) (-> String (Result a Error)) Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result a Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapDecodedWith f decode n opts)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (taskRun f n opts
        (lambda (r)
          {
            (vecPush out (taskDecodeResult decode r))
            0
          }
        )
        0)
      out
    }
  })

(:: taskDecodeResult (-> (-> String (Result a Error)) (Result String Error) (Result a Error)))
(fn (taskDecodeResult decode r)
  (match r
    ((Ok s) (decode s))
    ((Err e) (Err e))))

;; Fold the answers in submit order without keeping them: `step acc i r`
;; for each task, starting from `init`, answering the last `acc`.
;; Each answer and everything the step allocates are reclaimed on return.
;; A retained mark cell is refreshed before delivery, then reset after it.
;; The pool's state and the accumulator were allocated before that mark.
;
;; `scoped(step)` checks each invocation: supplied references and fresh
;; allocations cannot escape into captured state. Pass a lambda directly,
;; a non-nullary function declaration, or a parameter with the same tag.
;; The accumulator carries scalar bits. taskMapWith retains answers.
; The raw mark/reset remains this implementation's unsafe obligation.
;@axiom:scoped(step)
(pub :: taskFold (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts Int (-> Int Int (Result String Error) Int) Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskFold f n opts init step)
  {
    (let acc (taskScratch 16))
    (let mark (taskScratch 32))
    {
      (rawSetWord acc 0 init)
      (rawSetWord acc 1 0)
      (taskRun f n opts (lambda (r) (taskFoldOne acc step r)) mark)
      {
        (let v (rawGetWord acc 0))
        {
          (taskScratchDone acc)
          (taskScratchDone mark)
          v
        }
      }
    }
  })

; One step: word 0 of `acc` is the scalar accumulator, word 1 the
; submit-order index. Neither word may retain a scoped allocation.
(:: taskFoldOne (-> Int (-> Int Int (Result String Error) Int) (Result String Error) Int))
(fn (taskFoldOne acc step r)
  {
    (let i (rawGetWord acc 1))
    {
      (let g (step (rawGetWord acc 0) i))
      {
        (rawSetWord acc 1 (+ i 1))
        (rawSetWord acc 0 (g r))
      }
    }
  })

; ---- word results and external commands ------------------------------------
;; Run `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once,
;; answering the results in SUBMIT order.
;
; `hs` is a ring with at most min(n, w) slots, each a `Spawn`: the
; handle a spawn answers and its join takes (docs/memory-model.md
; MM-PAR-8), so a slot joined twice would trap with status 85 rather
; than read an unmapped page. Slot i % w is reused only after the old
; child in it has joined; `joined` counts submitted results, not
; positions in the ring. `out` still holds n results, so
; total result storage is O(n), while handle storage is O(min(n, w)).
; Capacity is capped by n before allocation: a huge width over a short
; or empty input does not ask for a huge ring. Vec clamps an empty or
; negative capacity to one word.
(pub :: taskMapWords (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapWords f n width)
  (wordPoolMap f n width))

; Join one `__proc_spawn` handle WITHOUT re-raising: `Ok` the thunk's
; word when the child exits 0, `Err` the child's wait status otherwise.
; `cell` is a live writable word owned by the calling pool. Every join
; overwrites it before returning, so one cell serves all sequential joins
; in that pool. It is never shared between concurrent pool invocations.
;
; PRIVATE, for `taskRunCommandWord`'s reason: the lossy-per-slot form is needed
; in one place, so it lives there, and a caller who wants the pool
; calls `taskMapWordsChecked`.
(:: taskWordsJoinChecked (-> Spawn Int (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskWordsJoinChecked h cell)
  {
    (let w (__proc_join_nr h cell))
    {
      (let st (rawGetWord cell 0))
      (if (== st 0)
        (Ok w)
        (Err (mkError st "parallel slot trapped")))
    }
  })

; Spawn `f` on `i` inside a recovery point of its own, so a refused
; spawn comes back here as a status rather than raising past the pool
; (MM-PAR-7's 78). Answers the handle's word, or the status: 78 when the
; kernel refused the fork or the handle table was full, 70 when the
; handle's page could not be mapped. The spawn is all the extent does,
; and the `region` around it takes back its one allocation, the mark.
(:: taskWordsSpawn (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
(fn (taskWordsSpawn f i)
  (region r
    (__axiom_recover __axiom_arena_mark (lambda (x) (taskWordOf (__proc_spawn f i))))))

; SIGKILL a running slot's child, through the pid its handle names. A
; child that has already exited is a zombie, and the signal changes
; nothing about it.
(:: taskWordsKill (-> Spawn Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskWordsKill h)
  (match (sysKill (__spawn_pid h) 9)
    ((Ok z) 0)
    ((Err e) 0)))

; Join a slot `taskWordsKill` just signalled: `Ok` its word when it had
; finished first, and otherwise `Err` its wait status, which is 137.
(:: taskWordsJoinKilled (-> Spawn Int (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskWordsJoinKilled h cell)
  {
    (let w (__proc_join_nr h cell))
    {
      (let st (rawGetWord cell 0))
      (if (== st 0)
        (Ok w)
        (Err (mkError st "parallel slot killed: a later spawn was refused")))
    }
  })

;; Run `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once,
;; answering one `Result` per slot in SUBMIT order: `Ok` the thunk's
;; word, `Err` the wait status of a slot whose thunk trapped.
;
; A SPAWN THAT IS REFUSED stops the pool. Its slot and every slot after
; it answer `Err` with the refusal's status - 78 when the kernel refused
; the fork or the handle table was full, 70 when no page could be mapped
; for the handle - and never run. Every slot still running is SIGKILLed
; and joined, in submit order, and answers what its join saw: `Ok` its
; word if it finished first, otherwise `Err` 137. So no child outlives
; the call, and the scratch cell and ring are released on this path
; too.
;
; The loop is `taskMapWords`'s, join for join - the width clamp, the
; block-before-spawning, the drain that continues `joined` - and the
; comments there are not repeated here. What differs is the join, the
; non-raising one, so a trapped slot is an `Err` in the answer and not
; the end of the program, and the spawn, which answers its refusal.
(pub :: taskMapWordsChecked (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int (Vec (Result Int Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapWordsChecked f n width)
  {
    (let w
      (if (< width 1)
        1
        width))
    (let hs
      (vecWithCapacity
        (if (< n w)
          n
          w)))
    (let out vecNew)
    (let cell (rawAlloc 8))
    (let mut joined 0)
    (let mut next 0)
    (let mut refused 0)
    {
      (__retain cell)
      (while (&& (< next n) (== refused 0))
        {
          (while (>= (- next joined) w)
            {
              (vecPush out (taskWordsJoinChecked (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          {
            (let hw (taskWordsSpawn f next))
            (if (taskSpawned hw)
              {
                {
                  (let h (taskHandleOf hw))
                  (if (< next w)
                    (vecPush hs h)
                    (vecSet hs (% next w) h))
                }
                (set next (+ next 1))
              }
              (set refused hw))
          }
        })
      (if (== refused 0)
        (while (< joined n)
          {
            (vecPush out (taskWordsJoinChecked (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
            (set joined (+ joined 1))
          })
        {
          (for k in joined..next
            (taskWordsKill (vecGet hs (% k w))))
          (while (< joined next)
            {
              (vecPush out (taskWordsJoinKilled (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          (for k in next..n
            (vecPush out
              (Err (mkError refused "parallel slot not started: its spawn was refused"))))
        })
      (__release cell)
      out
    }
  })

;; A null-terminated argv for `execve` or `posix_spawn`. Every string
;; passes through `strCStr`, which copies a slice when it needs a
;; terminator. The array lives in the caller's arena.
(pub :: taskArgvVector (-> (Vec String) Int))
(pub fn (taskArgvVector argv)
  (commandArgvVector argv))

;; Run one argument vector. Element 0 is the program, looked up on
;; `PATH`. An empty vector answers `Err` 2 without starting a process.
(pub :: taskRunCommand (-> (Vec String) (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskRunCommand argv)
  (commandRun argv))

;; Run argument vectors at up to `width` at once, in submit order.
;; Each slot contains its exit code, 128 plus its terminating signal,
;; or a negative errno when the program could not start.
(pub :: taskRunCommands (-> (Vec (Vec String)) Int (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskRunCommands cmds width)
  (commandPoolRun cmds width))

;; Map an Int-to-Int numeric computation with the ordinary Task budgets.
;; Results are submit-ordered. Each word crosses as exactly eight bytes;
;; the byte limit and keep budget apply to those bytes. Numeric conversion
;; is explicit. `compute(worker)` checks primitive captures and the entire
;; reachable numeric program, including local accumulators and helpers.
;@axiom:compute(worker)
(pub :: taskMapCompute (-> (-> Int Int) Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result Int Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapCompute worker count opts)
  (taskMapDecodedWith (lambda (index) (taskComputeEncode (worker index))) taskComputeDecode count
    opts))

; Private fixed-width transport. The allocated String owns eight writable
; bytes. Decoding checks the size before its one word load. No reference
; or hidden ownership promotion crosses the process boundary.
(:: taskComputeEncode (-> Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskComputeEncode word)
  {
    (let bytes (strAlloc 8))
    {
      (__store64 (strData bytes) 0 word)
      bytes
    }
  })

(:: taskComputeDecode (-> String (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskComputeDecode bytes)
  (if (== (strLen bytes) 8)
    (Ok (__load64 (strData bytes) 0))
    (Err (mkError 22 "task compute answer must contain exactly eight bytes"))))
